Z||000003||zeusrekning||08-08-2000||06:53 AM||||i want to build the engine for drag racing but want to be able to drive to the race.My girl friends dad is a mechanic. He has built chevy engine but he and i have different philoshopies. He likes to do it and get it done with no frills. I belive when you have an engine tore down you should do it to it the first time. I was thinking of buying the torker II system for edelbrock, it comes with carb, cam, lifters, and intake for about $588||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfixit58||08-08-2000||07:56 AM||||Have you ever heard the saying: "there's not replacement for displacement"? How about: "bigger is better"? The 455 ROCKS! Megga torque. The only knock the the motor is it's not a high reving motor. I runs out of breath between 4500 and 5500 RPM. But, gear it right and shift at the right times and it would be hard to beat. Remember the 350 and the 455 weigh the SAME! <P>If you want to dig into this subject, try: <A HREF="http://www.classicalpontiac.com/." TARGET=_blank>http://www.classicalpontiac.com/.</A>  Look in the Q&A section and ask a few questions. YOu won't believe how much info you'll get.<BR> <BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||10
Z||000005||Oreif||08-08-2000||11:45 AM||||If you'll notice in Mrfixit58's post he says that the 455 runs out of breath at around 5000 rpm. If you put on the Edlebrock Torker manifold you will lose low-end torque. The torker manifold is a single plane manifold and designed to "breathe" at high RPM's generally over 4000 RPM so you'll only have about 1000 RPM of good flow. I would stick with a dual-plane manifold especially since you want to drive it on the street. Dual-plane is better for a street/strip combo.<BR>||||reg||1
