Z||000007||zeusrekning||08-08-2000||08:01 PM||||i cant find no positive traction rear end. Can some one explain the diff. between posi traction and limited slip? I think i get it.posi trac. is like a live axle on a go kart. but a limited slip has a "limited amount of slip" before it catches???? ||||reg||1
Z||000008||olympic||08-08-2000||11:56 PM||||  I think "posi-trac" and "limited slip" were just different names given tot the same thing by different car makers.  If you're building an all out drag car you could look into a "locker" rear differential.  With this setup, whenever your car is travelling in a straight line the two axles are physically locked together for optimal traction.  When your car turns the axles unlock and act like a normal open differential.  Posi and LSD use clutches to transfer some of the power to each wheel and still allow the axles to turn seperately when needed.  But they are limited in how much power they can transfer.  One wheel still gets more power than the other.  With a locker diff, both wheels split the power evenly. <P>------------------<BR>"Those who can,  do.  Those who can't,  teach."||||reg||1
Z||000009||batboy||08-10-2000||04:57 PM||||If you like the idea of a matched Edelbrock system, then look into the Performer Plus setup. It'll give you better mid-range torque than the Torker II. It's hard to find good flowing heads for those big Pontiacs. Look for open chamber heads, especially the rare Ram Air IV GTO heads (haha, good luck).||||reg||1
Z||000010||zeusrekning||08-10-2000||07:43 PM||||Batboy? Please tell me you are batty. I thought all pontiac heads were good? But that was one of my othe ?'s How do i identify what heads are on the engine??<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||mrfixit58||08-11-2000||11:27 AM||||I wanted to respond to Batboy's comment yesterday but I had a headach the size of Mt Rushmore and just couldn't muster the effort.<P>No, all Pontiac heads are NOT junk. Some have larger chambers, some have smaller chambers. Some have large valves, some have smaller valves. The great thing about the Pontiac heads is from 1970 on, the flow design for the RAV head was used. So, You can spend LOTS of $$$ looking for RAV heads to make you Pontiac run, OR just buy later model heads for cheap. Just look for the right champer and valve combinations. And, unlike SBC, you can still find good heads in the junk yard (Shhh... it's a well kept secret).<P>Below is a link that can provide as much information on Pontiac motors as you can stand. If it's not enough, I encourage you to get on a Pontiac mailing list or a message board (such as this one for Fieros) and ask LOTS of questions. You'll learn a TON. I can recommend a few if you like. here's the link:<BR> <A HREF="http://www.angelfire.com/on/geebjen/pontiac.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.angelfire.com/on/geebjen/pontiac.html</A> <P>Good Luck,<BR>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||12
Z||000012||olympic||08-11-2000||08:14 PM||||  So you want slick rippling torque eh?  Heres what you do:<BR>  - Go to a junk yard and score a 1973-76 RWD Cadillac 501 and a set of 1968-1970 472-501 heads.<BR>  - Get the 501 short block rebuilt and balanced with forged pistons and premium fasteners.  Stock rods and crank are fine.<BR>  - rebuild the heads with over size big block chevy valves, stronger springs, 3 angle valve job, port/polish and hi-perf rocker arms.<BR>  - edelbrock makes a nice dual plane "Performer" intake for the 501.<BR>  - hi lift cams are available but I cant remember the place right now.<BR>  - header kits are available<P>  This combination will yield a 12.5:1 compression ratio and pump out 500 hp and 600 lbs of torque easily while weighing at least 50 lbs less than a 455.   And the 501 will bolt right up tp your TA's tranny!  If you want to be able to run pump gas use the 73-76 heads.  Mill them for ~9:1 CR and add a 250 shot of N2O   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>  The best part will be every time someone check out your engine you can tell them "Yeah.... It's a 500."   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
